<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/02EE">Warning <span data-tag="02EE">6.1.6.16</span>.</a> In the situation of Proposition <a data-tag="02ED" href="/tag/02ED">6.1.6.15</a>, it is possible for an object $X \in \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ to admit a weak right dual which is not a right dual. For example, let $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}= \operatorname{Vect}_{k}$ be the category of vector spaces over a field $k$, equipped with the monoidal structure of Example <a data-tag="00CQ" href="/tag/00CQ">2.1.3.1</a>. Let $V$ be a vector space over $k$ and let $V^{\ast } = \operatorname{Hom}_{k}(V, k)$ be its dual space. Then the evaluation map </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \operatorname{ev}: V \otimes _{k} V^{\ast } \rightarrow k \quad \quad v \otimes \lambda \mapsto \lambda (v) \]
</div>
<p> exhibits $V^{\ast }$ as a weak (right) dual of $V$ (in the sense of Definition <a data-tag="02EB" href="/tag/02EB">6.1.6.13</a>). However, it is a duality datum only when $V$ is finite-dimensional over $k$ (Exercise <a data-tag="02EA" href="/tag/02EA">6.1.6.12</a>). </p>
</article>
